  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Considering the User\'s Intent**\n\nI\'m delving into the user\'s prompt, specifically the \"Part 3\" context. It\'s a continuation, so I must consider prior elements and their flow. The imagery of a dragon, an old king, and a stolen cup is rich with potential. I\'m focusing on how to maintain the existing page\'s structure while injecting more nuanced, less AI-driven content.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Narrative\'s Thread**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the implications of the dragon rampage and Beowulf\'s final stand. I\'m trying to interpret the user\'s intent. Do they want a Beowulf-themed adaptation of the current design, or is it unrelated? I need to clarify the relationship between this narrative and the initial prompt, while considering the \"keep the\" instruction. Is the Beowulf text a thematic element, or a directive to implement that specific content?\n\n\n**Interpreting User Intent**\n\nI\'m now grappling with the user\'s intent concerning the \"Part 3\" context and the Beowulf narrative. Are they building a Beowulf-themed interactive experience, or are they integrating a specific Beowulf passage? The instruction to maintain the \"good hero page\" aesthetic adds another layer. I\'m trying to decipher if the Beowulf text serves as thematic inspiration, a direct content insertion, or a starting point for a different concept. The options are stacking.\n\n\n**Deciphering User\'s Vision**\n\nI\'m now weighing two possibilities: a full transformation into a high-end Beowulf experience, or integrating Beowulf as a dedicated \"epic\" mode within the existing design. The phrase \"keep the same good hero page and make it less AI slop\" is key. I\'m focusing on the request to retain the current page\'s essence while injecting the narrative, thinking about the implications for page layout and content integration. I\'m also considering if the user wants an epic story reader.\n\n\n**Clarifying Dual Intent**\n\nI\'m now separating two potential user requests: maintaining the existing \"good hero page\" aesthetic and incorporating the Beowulf narrative. I\'m considering if the user intends a combined experience or a toggle between them. Is the goal an interactive epic *within* the current design, or a separate experience? I\'m re-evaluating whether the prompt combines two separate thoughts, or just one. I must find which of those two intents applies, because a failure in either is a failure of the overall prompt.\n\n\n**Unpacking Beowulf\'s Purpose**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the user\'s possible intent, connecting \"Part 3\" with the Beowulf narrative. The \"keep the same good hero page and make it less ai slop\" instruction is making me think. I\'m focusing on two possible directions. I believe the user wants a literature project or application that mirrors the editorial aesthetic, or a section within the original application, that serves the function of an epic saga. I am going back and forth on these two potential intentions, and will pick a more focused course shortly.\n\n\n**Refining Possible Applications**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the two potential applications stemming from the user\'s intent. The possibilities are: an interactive web experience inspired by \"Beowulf: Part 3: The Dragon and the Old King,\" or an application mirroring the original page\'s layout but adapted for the epic. I\'m focusing on the user\'s \"keep the same good hero page\" instruction and wondering if the real estate scanner could be included.\n\n\n**Synthesizing User Wishes**\n\nI\'m now integrating the user\'s latest context. I see the potential for a \"scanner,\" the original real estate page, alongside \"The Dragon & The Old King\" saga, both accessible via a toggle. I\'m considering if the user might want a completely AI-free version of the original \"good hero page\". I\'m rethinking the user\'s prompt as the driving consideration, and parsing their intent. I\'m focusing on the safest, most delightful, and most impressive solution. I\'m aiming for seamless integration of both components, prioritizing a zero-AI feel.\n\n\n**Formulating Possible Layouts**\n\nI\'m now thinking about the user\'s focus. The user is focused on *both* the interactive Beowulf experience and the STR scanner. This should be combined, rather than two separate items. I\'m focusing on the switcher in the top bar, with its pencil icon and the saga title. The user wants a deeply crafted, tactile experience. This focus on \"no AI slop\" gives me clarity. I\'m prioritizing integrating the components.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Central Prompt**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on a pivotal interpretation. What if the user wants the \"Part 3\" narrative to *replace* the existing hero page? The user\'s phrasing, \"keep the same good hero page,\" suggests preserving its structure. It could be an adaptation. It\'s a key possibility. I\'m imagining a complete overhaul of the hero page using the saga\'s structure. The goal is to fully deliver on the intent.\n\n\n**Designing the Dual Experiences**\n\nI\'m now integrating all these ideas. I\'m imagining a top bar toggle. One mode is the existing hero page. The other mode, accessed via a button labeled \"\360\237\217\260 The Dragon & The Old King (Part 3)\", will be a beautiful Beowulf experience. This design mirrors the existing hero page perfectly. The saga section will contain a Heroic Chronicle badge, with an italicized, amber title. I see the search bar becoming an epic action terminal, with verse exploration. I will now start structuring these elements in a prototype.\n\n\n**Constructing Interactive Modules**\n\nI\'m now building out interactive modules inspired by the user\'s focus on Beowulf. I\'m imagining a Dragon\'s Barrow scan, complete with interactive elements like a Hoard Drop tag. I\'m focusing on the Battle Strategy Selector and Combat Metrics to bring the saga to life. I want to build a truly tactile experience, so I\'m considering components like the Scrubbers, the Oath Banner, and the Codex Memoir. I am hoping to create a full interactive epic for this user.\n\n\n**Developing Interactive Features**\n\nI\'m now prioritizing the user\'s focus on interactivity. I\'m building out a \"Module 03\" about Naegling\'s Bite, complete with case studies and epic tiers. The user\'s directive is front and center! I\'m planning to craft a \"Carve the memorial\" call to action. I\'m also ensuring easy access to the original underwriting terminal, with a sleek, segmented switch in the top header, which is essential.\n\n\n**Defining Core Beowulf Components**\n\nI\'m now identifying the key files for the Beowulf project. I need to create data structures and modules. I will use authentic text, and interactive elements. I am prioritizing a robust, adaptable, and genuinely engaging experience. I will include a real interface, authentic data, and modular components to achieve the user\'s vision.\n\n\n"
    tool_calls {
