  planner_response {
    response: "### What Was Built: \"Part 3: The Dragon and the Old King \360\237\220\211\"\n\nWe adapted the visual layout of the original hero page (warm limestone aesthetic, split-screen live monitor terminal, data-dense status indicators, feature modules, and bottom obsidian banner) into an authentic, scholarly, and non-slop interactive codex for **Beowulf: Part 3 \342\200\224 The Dragon and the Old King**, while keeping the **PencilSTR Deal Terminal** instantly toggleable in the top bar.\n\n---\n\n### Key Features of Part 3: The Dragon and the Old King\n\n1. **Earnaness Live Battle & Encounter Terminal**\n   - **Hero Editorial Headline & Verse Search**: *\342\200\234Fifty winters as King of the Geats. The dragon stirs in smoke and flame.\342\200\235* Interactive search bar supporting line lookups (e.g., \302\2472215, \302\2472633, \302\2472680, \302\2472794) and quick benchmark tags.\n   - **Visual Barrow Monitor**: Ancient barrow photography with the **-$1 Golden Flagon Stolen** tag, elevation indicators, and Geatish retinue specs (`Earnaness Sea-Promontory | King: 78 Winters | 10 Thanes Deserted | Wiglaf Standing`).\n   - **12-Phase Devastation Timeline**: Interactive bar chart detailing the arc from 300 winters of slumber to the theft of the flagon, the burning of the king\'s mead-hall, the dragon\'s venomous neck strike, and the funeral pyre at Hronesness.\n   - **Combat Model Selector**: Toggle between *Solo Duel*, *Wiglaf\'s Flank (Canonical)*, and *Iron Shield Defense*.\n   - **Dynamic Battle Scrubbers**:\n     - *Dragon Fire Core Temperature (1,400\302\260F \342\200\223 2,800\302\260F)*: Dynamically calculates linden wood shield meltdown rate and N\303\246gling blade shatter probability.\n     - *Thane Comitatus Loyalty Index*: Visual breakdown showing the 10 deserters vs. Wiglaf alone.\n\n2. **The Three Deconstructed Codex Modules**\n   - **Module 01: The Theft of the Golden Flagon (\302\2472215)**: The runaway thrall who stole the jeweled beaker to appease his angry master, waking the barrow.\n   - **Module 02: Comitatus Oath Breakdown (\302\2472633)**: Capital stack breakdown of the war-band and the dishonor of the ten retainers who fled into the Earnaness woods.\n   - **Module 03: The Shattering of N\303\246gling (\302\2472680)**: How Beowulf\'s excessive strength caused the ancient grey-etched blade to snap upon the dragon\'s skull-bone.\n\n3. **Heroic Chronicles & Memorials**\n   - **The Slaying at Earnaness**: Dual strike audit (Wiglaf\'s blade into the lower belly, Beowulf\'s seam-knife cut, and the fatal venom).\n   - **The Memorial at Hronesness**: Ten-day mound construction on the sea-cliff headland where the gold was re-buried into the earth.\n\n4. **The Three Figures of the Saga**\n   - **Role 01 / The Runaway Thrall**: The accidental catalyst of the war.\n   - **Role 02 / King Beowulf (Most Noble)**: Wayland ring-mail, iron buckler, and the king who traded his life for his people\'s hoard.\n   - **Role 03 / Wiglaf (The Successor)**: Son of Weohstan, inheritor of the golden collar, war-helm, and future protector of Geatland.\n\n5. **Interactive Modals & Sc\305\215p Chant Simulation**\n   - **Beowulf\'s Dying Testament (\302\2472794\342\200\223\302\2472820)**: Printable bilingual codex view with Old English and Modern English text of the king\'s final speech, naming Wiglaf his successor.\n   - **Verse Reader & Sc\305\215p Chant**: Line-by-line inspection with simulated recitation of Old English alliterative verse.\n   - **The Ten Thanes Comitatus Audit**: Wiglaf\'s pronouncement of landless exile upon the coward retainers.\n\n6. **Seamless Mode Toggle**\n   - Easily switch between **The Dragon & King** and the **PencilSTR Underwriting Suite** via the pill toggle in the top navigation bar."
    modified_response: "### What Was Built: \"Part 3: The Dragon and the Old King \360\237\220\211\"\n\nWe adapted the visual layout of the original hero page (warm limestone aesthetic, split-screen live monitor terminal, data-dense status indicators, feature modules, and bottom obsidian banner) into an authentic, scholarly, and non-slop interactive codex for **Beowulf: Part 3 \342\200\224 The Dragon and the Old King**, while keeping the **PencilSTR Deal Terminal** instantly toggleable in the top bar.\n\n---\n\n### Key Features of Part 3: The Dragon and the Old King\n\n1. **Earnaness Live Battle & Encounter Terminal**\n   - **Hero Editorial Headline & Verse Search**: *\342\200\234Fifty winters as King of the Geats. The dragon stirs in smoke and flame.\342\200\235* Interactive search bar supporting line lookups (e.g., \302\2472215, \302\2472633, \302\2472680, \302\2472794) and quick benchmark tags.\n   - **Visual Barrow Monitor**: Ancient barrow photography with the **-$1 Golden Flagon Stolen** tag, elevation indicators, and Geatish retinue specs (`Earnaness Sea-Promontory | King: 78 Winters | 10 Thanes Deserted | Wiglaf Standing`).\n   - **12-Phase Devastation Timeline**: Interactive bar chart detailing the arc from 300 winters of slumber to the theft of the flagon, the burning of the king\'s mead-hall, the dragon\'s venomous neck strike, and the funeral pyre at Hronesness.\n   - **Combat Model Selector**: Toggle between *Solo Duel*, *Wiglaf\'s Flank (Canonical)*, and *Iron Shield Defense*.\n   - **Dynamic Battle Scrubbers**:\n     - *Dragon Fire Core Temperature (1,400\302\260F \342\200\223 2,800\302\260F)*: Dynamically calculates linden wood shield meltdown rate and N\303\246gling blade shatter probability.\n     - *Thane Comitatus Loyalty Index*: Visual breakdown showing the 10 deserters vs. Wiglaf alone.\n\n2. **The Three Deconstructed Codex Modules**\n   - **Module 01: The Theft of the Golden Flagon (\302\2472215)**: The runaway thrall who stole the jeweled beaker to appease his angry master, waking the barrow.\n   - **Module 02: Comitatus Oath Breakdown (\302\2472633)**: Capital stack breakdown of the war-band and the dishonor of the ten retainers who fled into the Earnaness woods.\n   - **Module 03: The Shattering of N\303\246gling (\302\2472680)**: How Beowulf\'s excessive strength caused the ancient grey-etched blade to snap upon the dragon\'s skull-bone.\n\n3. **Heroic Chronicles & Memorials**\n   - **The Slaying at Earnaness**: Dual strike audit (Wiglaf\'s blade into the lower belly, Beowulf\'s seam-knife cut, and the fatal venom).\n   - **The Memorial at Hronesness**: Ten-day mound construction on the sea-cliff headland where the gold was re-buried into the earth.\n\n4. **The Three Figures of the Saga**\n   - **Role 01 / The Runaway Thrall**: The accidental catalyst of the war.\n   - **Role 02 / King Beowulf (Most Noble)**: Wayland ring-mail, iron buckler, and the king who traded his life for his people\'s hoard.\n   - **Role 03 / Wiglaf (The Successor)**: Son of Weohstan, inheritor of the golden collar, war-helm, and future protector of Geatland.\n\n5. **Interactive Modals & Sc\305\215p Chant Simulation**\n   - **Beowulf\'s Dying Testament (\302\2472794\342\200\223\302\2472820)**: Printable bilingual codex view with Old English and Modern English text of the king\'s final speech, naming Wiglaf his successor.\n   - **Verse Reader & Sc\305\215p Chant**: Line-by-line inspection with simulated recitation of Old English alliterative verse.\n   - **The Ten Thanes Comitatus Audit**: Wiglaf\'s pronouncement of landless exile upon the coward retainers.\n\n6. **Seamless Mode Toggle**\n   - Easily switch between **The Dragon & King** and the **PencilSTR Underwriting Suite** via the pill toggle in the top navigation bar."
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
